/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   GUESS THE MAP — the built-in maps
   Every one is drawn by the app itself from real borders (maps-geo.js: Natural
   Earth 1:10m countries, US Census states), so all twenty share one look —
   same sea, same land, same palette, same legend — and every border, coast and
   city dot sits where it really is. Nothing is a traced or approximated image.

   A map is pure data:
     view   which base map — world · europe · northatl · namerica · us
     ata    world only: keep Antarctica (cropped off otherwise)
     cls    shaded groups: { c:colour, codes:'ISO3 …', label:at reveal, hint:during play }
     rest   optional fill for every country NOT in a group (the "other half")
     labels text printed on a country throughout — every number map shows its
            numbers during play, not just at the reveal (house rule: the
            numbers are the clue; without them the hard maps are guesswork)
     rlabels text printed on a country only at the reveal
     pts    [lat, lon, name, [dx, dy, anchor]] — exact coordinates, name shown at reveal
     lines  parallels drawn at the reveal (the Equator)
     level  easy / medium / hard — Setup can play one or several
     topic  the one hint, a subject area ("History", "Sport") shown only when
            the phone holder presses Hint — it narrows the field, never names it
   The title is the answer, so it never appears until the reveal. A `hint` is
   allowed to tell the room what the colours are without saying what they mean
   — "70–100" on the corruption map is fair; "least corrupt" is not.
   Facts are sourced in each `notes`, and that note is what the host reads out.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
